    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, ruled in its last century from Pakuan Pajajaran on the site of modern Bogor.</li><li>It exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a>, the port that became Jakarta, and resisted both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and the Muslim coastal states.</li><li>Banten’s capture of Pakuan in 1579 ended the kingdom, but Sundanese language, literature and identity endure in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The stone pillar set up for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-strait">Sunda</a>’s 1522 treaty with Portugal was rediscovered in Jakarta in 1918 and is now kept in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

