    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sri-baduga-maharaja"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja.jpg" alt="Sri Baduga Maharaja" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Siliwangi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ridho, a painter from Sancang village, Cibalong sub-district, Garut district., CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sunda Kingdom occupied the western third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, the land of the Sundanese people, which in the fifth century had been the seat of Tarumanagara, known from the inscriptions of King Purnavarman. Later Sundanese tradition dates the kingdom’s foundation to 669, when Tarusbawa is said to have taken the western part of the former Tarumanagara lands while the eastern part, beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#citarum-river">Citarum River</a>, became the Galuh Kingdom. This account comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> written centuries afterwards, some of disputed authenticity, and the early history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-strait">Sunda</a> remains largely uncertain. The earliest securely dated record naming a king of Sunda is the Kebon Kopi II inscription near Bogor, generally read as dated to 932, and the Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions of 1030 near Sukabumi record a ruler called Sri Jayabupati who declared a stretch of river sacred and forbade fishing there. The kingdom’s centre shifted between Pakuan, near modern Bogor, and Kawali in the Galuh lands, where inscriptions commemorate the long-reigning king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niskala-wastu-kancana">Niskala Wastu Kancana</a> in the fifteenth century. The crowns of Sunda and Galuh were often held by one ruler, and around 1482 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a> united them permanently and ruled from Pakuan Pajajaran. By the early sixteenth century the Portuguese apothecary Tomé Pires described Sunda as a kingdom extending from the Sunda Strait east to the Cimanuk River.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sunda-kelapa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa.jpg" alt="Sunda Kelapa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kelapa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pinisi_Sunda_Kelapa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Danumurthi Mahendra from Indonesia, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-strait">Sunda</a> was an agrarian kingdom, but its farming differed from that of central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>. Much of the Sundanese highland was cultivated by shifting dry-field <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> farming, known as <em>huma</em>, rather than by the irrigated rice terraces that sustained dense populations further east, and the population was correspondingly thinner. The kingdom’s great export was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a>. The Chinese official Zhao Rugu noted around 1225 that Sunda produced pepper of high quality, and Tomé Pires, writing about 1512–1515, listed its ports, among them Banten, Pontang, Tangerang, Kalapa and Cimanuk, which shipped pepper, rice and other goods to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malacca">Malacca</a> and beyond. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a>, at the mouth of the Ciliwung River, was the most important harbour and served the capital upriver. Religion combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist teaching with older Sundanese reverence for ancestors and sacred places, the <em>kabuyutan</em>, which were protected sites of worship and learning. Old Sundanese literature preserves this world: the <em>Sanghyang Siksa Kandang Karesian</em>, dated 1518, sets out moral and practical knowledge for different ranks of society, and the poem of Bujangga Manik, a Sundanese prince turned hermit who travelled through Java and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bali">Bali</a>, describes the landscape and religious sites of the late fifteenth century. Its palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscript</a> has been held in the Bodleian Library at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oxford">Oxford</a> since 1627.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#gajah-mada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gajah-mada.jpg" alt="Gajah Mada" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gajah_Mada_in_Relief_of_Indonesian_History,_Monas.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gunawan Kartapranata, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Little is known of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-strait">Sunda</a>’s armies, but they were land forces drawn from the nobility and their followers, and the kingdom does not appear to have maintained a significant war fleet. Its most famous military episode is a tragedy. In 1357, according to the Javanese <em>Pararaton</em> and the later poem <em>Kidung Sunda</em>, King Linggabuana travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> with his daughter Dyah Pitaloka, who was to marry King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>. When the Majapahit chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> demanded that the princess be presented as a tribute of submission rather than as a bride, the Sundanese party refused and was destroyed in the Battle of Bubat; the princess is said to have taken her own life. Sunda nonetheless appears never to have been brought under Majapahit rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a> strengthened Pakuan Pajajaran, and the Batutulis inscription records that he surrounded it with a moat. The decisive threat of the sixteenth century came from Muslim states on the north coast. In 1522 Sunda made a treaty with the Portuguese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malacca">Malacca</a>, allowing them to build a fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a> in return for help, but before the Portuguese returned, forces from Demak and Cirebon under Fatahillah captured Banten and, in 1527, Sunda Kalapa itself, defeating a Portuguese landing party. The kingdom fought on inland for half a century against the new Banten Sultanate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sunda-strait"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sunda-strait.jpg" alt="Sunda Strait" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sunda-strait">Sunda Strait</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunda_strait_map_v4.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Telim tor, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-strait">Sunda</a> was ruled by a king styled <em>prabu</em> or <em>maharaja</em>, whose authority rested on lineage, on religious sanction and on the loyalty of regional lords. The dual crowns of Sunda and Galuh reflected a long-standing division between west and east, and later Sundanese chronicles describe frequent shifts of the royal seat and succession by sons, brothers and sons-in-law. Inscriptions show kings regulating sacred lands: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> plates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a> found at Kebantenan exempt religious communities from taxes and labour dues, and the Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions protect a sacred stretch of river under threat of curses. The Batutulis inscription of 1533, set up by Sri Baduga’s son Surawisesa, praises his father for building the moat of Pakuan, raising an artificial hill, creating a sacred forest and constructing a lake, works that combined defence, ritual and public display. Popular tradition identifies Sri Baduga with the legendary Prabu Siliwangi, the model king of Sundanese storytelling, though the identification cannot be proven. Ports were governed by royal officials who collected dues from traders, and outlying areas such as Cirebon and Sumedang Larang were held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> lords. Cirebon, ruled from the late fifteenth century by the Muslim teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunan-gunung-jati">Sunan Gunung Jati</a>, broke away by refusing tribute, an early sign of how coastal conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> could detach the kingdom’s richest territories from its control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sunda Kingdom shaped the identity of the Sundanese, today one of the largest ethnic groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indonesia">Indonesia</a>, who form the majority of the population of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a> and Banten. Old Sundanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> and inscriptions, from the Kawali stones to the poem of Bujangga Manik, are the foundation of Sundanese literary history, and the figure of Prabu Siliwangi remains central to Sundanese oral tradition, the <em>pantun</em> recited by bards. The Indonesian army’s West Java command bears the name Siliwangi. The kingdom’s capital lay on the site of modern Bogor, and Batutulis still stands in the city. Its principal port, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a>, became Jayakarta after the Muslim conquest and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#batavia">Batavia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a>, and Jakarta dates its foundation to 22 June 1527, the traditional date of Fatahillah’s victory. The stone pillar, or <em>padrão</em>, erected for the 1522 treaty with Portugal was rediscovered in Jakarta in 1918 and is kept in the National Museum of Indonesia, one of the earliest records of European diplomacy in the archipelago. The memory of the Battle of Bubat long coloured relations between Sundanese and Javanese. Some communities, notably the Baduy of the Banten highlands, maintain an ancestral religion often linked to pre-Islamic Sundanese belief, and scholars use their customs to help interpret the kingdom’s religious practice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-strait">Sunda</a>’s decline followed the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> along the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a> in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. Muslim merchants and teachers established communities in the ports, and once Cirebon had broken away and Demak had become the leading power of central Java, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom lost control of the harbours that provided its trade revenue. The Portuguese alliance of 1522 came too late to help, and the fall of Banten and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kalapa</a> around 1526–1527 cut Sunda off from the sea and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a> trade, which passed to the new Banten Sultanate under Hasanuddin. Surawisesa, who reigned until about 1535, is credited in Sundanese tradition with fighting many battles to defend the remaining realm, but his successors ruled a landlocked and shrinking kingdom. In 1579 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maulana-yusuf">Maulana Yusuf</a> of Banten captured and destroyed Pakuan Pajajaran. According to tradition the stone throne of the Sundanese kings was carried off to Banten, where a coronation stone still lies at the old palace site of Surosowan, and the last king, Raga Mulya, died in the western hills soon afterwards. Some Sundanese traditions hold that the royal regalia passed to Sumedang Larang, which became the main Sundanese successor in the east until it came under Mataram. West Java was thereafter divided between Banten, Cirebon and Mataram and later fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> rule.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sunda Kingdom was the last major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> state of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a>-exporting realm that stayed outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> control and made the 1522 treaty with Portugal, and whose fall to Banten in 1579 ended pre-Islamic kingship in the region while leaving a lasting Sundanese language, literature and identity.</em></p>

